%% ============================================================
%%  IV. DEEP-LEARNING SIDE-CHANNEL ANALYSIS
%% ============================================================
\section{Deep-Learning Side-Channel Analysis}
\label{sec:dlsca}

\subsection{The Unmasked Core Leaks First-Order}

Profiling CNN and MLP models on the round-1 S-box target — one
model per column, 6~HW classes, 80/20 train/validation split
over random-key captures — reaches 28--35\% top-1 validation
accuracy against a 16.7\% chance floor. The best per-column
profiles reach 38.5\% on column~0, well above the
majority-class floor. The leakage generalizes: models trained
on one set of random keys validate on held-out traces with
fresh keys. In absolute SNR the signal is approximately
$-$19\,dB at the VCCINT shunt, consistent with the core being a
small fraction of die activity.

\subsection{Profiling-Domain Gap and Live On-Board Fine-Tuning}

Profiles trained off-line transfer poorly to the live board:
the profiling domain differs in noise color, alignment
residual, and gain structure, causing closed-loop attacks to
mis-converge. The fix is live on-board fine-tuning: at a known
key, a small set of traces is captured with random nonces and
the profile is fine-tuned on the board's own leakage. With only
300 on-board traces the profile reaches 90.7\% training
accuracy against a 72\% majority floor, closing the domain gap.
This is the practical recipe for attacking deployed devices with
profiling access.

\subsection{Closed-Loop Adaptive Key Recovery}

With the board-fine-tuned profile, a closed-loop adaptive
chosen-nonce attack on one S-box column — posterior scoring
over the 4 key-bit hypotheses, $M\!=\!1$ per query — converges
to the correct hypothesis in 32 queries with posterior 0.999.
The attack uses only public information: power traces and chosen
nonces. The scope of this result is stated honestly: it is
per-column key recovery (2 of 128 bits), repeated across
columns for full-key assembly (Section~\ref{sec:synthesis}).

\subsection{Nonce-Repetition Averaging as the SNR Lever}

Capturing the same separating nonce $M$ times and averaging
raises per-query SNR by $\sqrt{M}$. A dedicated on-board probe
measured the effect on the true hypothesis rank for a single
column: rank-1 probability 0.25 at $M\!=\!1$ (chance) rising
to 0.94 at $M\!=\!64$. In the closed loop, $M\!=\!64$ shortens
convergence from 15 to 9 queries. The pipeline's
align-then-normalize order removes jitter and DC drift, and the
rank and posterior metrics show the full $\sqrt{M}$ gain.

\subsection{Where the Leakage Lives}

A localization study found the dominant key-dependent activity
is a byte-serial transfer at a regular 16-sample spacing inside
the FPGA die — the host-to-register-file load path. The crypto
rounds themselves sit below the first-order floor at this
measurement point. In a deployed device that loads its key once
and encrypts many nonces, the load-path activity is constant
across queries and carries no per-query key information; what
remains first-order observable is the round-1 S-box leakage
described above.

%% ============================================================
%%  V. MASKED-CORE COMPARISON
%% ============================================================
\section{Masked-Core Comparison}
\label{sec:masked}

Running the identical pipeline against a $d\!=\!1$ (2-share)
threshold-implemented ASCON core (9{,}151 traces, random keys)
places the round-1 S-box target at chance for every tested
architecture (CNN, MLP, centered-product MLP): first-order
masking effectiveness is confirmed on hardware. Two further
observations complete the picture.

The non-factorable KADD intermediate leaks at approximately
$2\times$ chance on the masked core (21.6\% vs.\ 11.1\%,
centered-product MLP, all 8 bytes, held-out random keys) — a
real, generalizing signal — but it does not support search: the
true key scores only $+3.1$\,nats/trace above random keys, and
beam search cannot find it because KADD depends on all 128 key
bits through 12 rounds of diffusion with no per-byte
factorization and no local gradient.

The structural picture on the masked core: the factorable target
(S-box) is untrainable; the trainable target (KADD) is
unsearchable. On the unmasked core the factorable target becomes
trainable — which is why the partial-recovery results of
Section~\ref{sec:dlsca} exist — and the remaining gap is
coverage across all 64 columns rather than signal strength.

%% ============================================================
%%  VI. FAULT INJECTION
%% ============================================================
\section{Fault Injection and Single-Bit Fault Localization}
\label{sec:fi}

\subsection{Setup}

Clock glitches are injected from the Husky scope into the
10\,MHz crypto clock, parameterized by glitch offset from
trigger ($e_o$, in ADC-sample units), sub-offset (\textit{off}),
glitch width ($w$), and glitch count per encryption. Each query
uses a fixed key and a random nonce. A query is faulted if its
tag readback differs from the fault-free oracle for the same
(key, nonce). Faulty tags are then localized off-line: with the
key known, we simulate a round-11 single-bit flip at each of
the 320 state-bit positions and check which (if any) single
flip reproduces the observed faulty tag exactly.

\subsection{Parameter Sweep and Band Structure}

Sweeping $e_o$ over 28--50 reveals a stable fault band at
$e_o\!=\!41$ with two landing positions ($\textit{off} = 0$ and
3359). The band yields large tag differentials (27--41 bit
differences); adjacent offsets ($e_o\!=\!42$--47) yield small
differentials (5--11 bits). From 406 band hits over 153
distinct tags, 12 clean single-bit faults localized to exact
(column, bit) positions, as shown in Table~\ref{tab:faults}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \caption{Round-11 Single-Bit Faults Localized to Exact
  (Column, Bit). All at $e_o\!=\!41$, $w\!=\!67$, one glitch;
  each verified by exact faulty-tag match under the
  simulated flip.}
  \label{tab:faults}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{cccc}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Nonce (prefix)} & \textbf{Sub-offset} &
    \textbf{$\Delta$bits} &
    \textbf{Col / Bit} \\
    \midrule
    8b4ae5f1 & 0    & 37 & 41 / 1 \\
    62f90a94 & 0    & 23 & 24 / 2 \\
    70aca1e9 & 3359 & 31 & 15 / 2 \\
    48dc03d1 & 0    & 19 & 24 / 2 \\
    1ef7677a & 0    & 33 & 41 / 1 \\
    b72f45b3 & 0    & 16 & 41 / 1 \\
    e9e15a77 & 0    & 16 & 39 / 2 \\
    6dcb81db & 0    & 16 & 41 / 1 \\
    7179ced2 & 0    & 16 & 39 / 2 \\
    85452a44 & 0    & 25 &  6 / 1 \\
    83bc45fe & 3359 & 22 & 52 / 2 \\
    22e791b8 & 0    & 15 & 18 / 4 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Deterministic Output-Path Fault Class}

At $e_o\!=\!42$ the glitch produces a distinct, deterministic
fault class: 7-bit differentials (5--11 bits across nonces)
with stable structure, localized to the output path — the fault
lands post-permutation, corrupting the readback path rather
than the round-11 state. This second, precisely-placed fault
class is useful for protocol-level fault studies even though it
carries no key information.

\subsection{Burst Faults and Round-Skip Attempts}

Multi-glitch bursts (num\_glitches\,=\,4 at the band center)
fault every query (400/400 faulted) but as multi-bit
superpositions: tag differentials span 15--16 bytes (median
30 bits), and none of the 400 faults matches any single-bit
model. Two-fault superposition models and round-skip hypotheses
likewise do not reproduce any observed tag. The measured
structure of burst faults is genuinely multi-bit within the
round datapath — not composable single-bit faults, and not
round skips.

\subsection{Fault-Placement Determinism and DFA Consequence}

The fault placement is data-selected: each nonce faults exactly
one column, reproducibly — nonce \texttt{8b4ae5...} always
faults column~41 bit~1 across re-sweeps of offset, width,
clock rate, and core voltage — while different nonces select
different single columns. The 12 localizations span 10 distinct
columns. For DFA this has a precise consequence: classical
full-key recovery needs the round-11 state of a single query
faulted across many columns; with one column per nonce and
state-varying nonces, per-query coverage is structurally bounded
at one column. The fault-coverage objective for full-key DFA
is therefore either (a)~nonce-set engineering against the
data-selected placement function, or (b)~multi-glitch schedules
placing several single-bit faults in one query — both supported
by the platform.

%% ============================================================
%%  VII. LEAKAGE-VALIDATION GATES
%% ============================================================
\section{Leakage-Validation Gates}
\label{sec:gates}

We report five artifact classes that generated confident false
positives during this campaign. We present each as a reusable
gate because these traps are platform-general and, to our
knowledge, undocumented for LWC hardware evaluation pipelines.

\subsection{Trap 1: Prior-Driven Edge Scores}

A per-trace log-likelihood edge metric reported a confident
$+0.398$-nat edge on data where logistic, kNN, and CNN
classifiers all sat at the majority-class floor. The edge
measured the class prior, not per-trace discrimination.
\textbf{Gate:} leakage claims must be backed by held-out label
accuracy beating the column's majority-class floor by a margin.

\subsection{Trap 2: Prior-Only Profiles and Separating-Nonce
Selection}

A profile whose validation accuracy sits below the majority
floor outputs the prior regardless of trace content; an
adaptive loop that picks maximally separating nonces then
deterministically selects the nonce pair whose accidental
behavior inverts the prior's preference, producing confident
wrong convergence (posterior 0.993, true hypothesis label match
0\%). \textbf{Gate:} every profile entering a closed loop must
clear the majority-floor gate; separating-nonce selection
amplifies priors as readily as signals.

\subsection{Trap 3: Public-Nonce Shortcut Labels}

At a fixed key, the round-1 S-box labels of any column are a
deterministic function of two public nonce bits. A model that
reads nonce-load activity in the trace predicts these labels
perfectly with zero secret involvement. \textbf{Gate:}
profiling must use random keys, and any claimed key recovery
must verify against a different key or against public-output
re-encryption.

\subsection{Trap 4: Key-Reload Register-Bus Artifact}

A capture loop that rewrites the key to the device register
file before every trace embeds the key-write bus activity in
every trace. The resulting correlation reaches $|r|\approx0.6$
at the load samples and appears at up to 64/64 columns as pure
key-bit leakage. This is the strongest apparent signal on the
platform and it is entirely a capture-construction artifact.
\textbf{Gate:} any key-value-dependent leak must survive a
fixed-key capture (key constant, nonces varying), in which the
reload term vanishes from correlation.

\subsection{Trap 5: Structured-Key and Free-Alignment False
Recovery}

A per-bit template attack with a free alignment offset,
evaluated against the counter-ramp key
\texttt{000102...0f}, reported 75.8\% per-bit recovery —
5.6~standard deviations above a random-key null. The
reversed ramp scores identically (75.8\%) and the fully
bit-inverted key scores higher (76.6\%), exposing the artifact:
a genuine read must invert to $\approx24\%$. A blind random-key
capture through the identical pipeline shows no signal at any
roll (60.2\% over a $\pm800$-sample scan).
\textbf{Gate:} inversion-invariance plus structure-preserving
nulls plus a blind random-key capture through the identical
pipeline as the final control before any recovery claim.

%% ============================================================
%%  VIII. ATTACK-SURFACE SYNTHESIS
%% ============================================================
\section{Attack-Surface Synthesis}
\label{sec:synthesis}

The measured picture across both arms is as follows.

\textbf{Unmasked core, power analysis:} the factorable round-1
S-box target is trainable (28--35\% top-1); live fine-tuning
closes the domain gap; per-column closed-loop recovery is
demonstrated (posterior 0.999, 32 queries); $\sqrt{M}$
averaging is the SNR lever (rank-1 $0.25\!\to\!0.94$ at
$M\!=\!64$). The remaining gap is assembling all 64 columns.

\textbf{Unmasked core, fault injection:} verified single-bit
round-11 faults exist and localize exactly (12 cases, 10
columns); burst faults are multi-bit; placement is
data-selected at one column per nonce. The remaining gap is
intra-query column coverage.

\textbf{Masked core:} the factorable target is suppressed to
chance (9{,}151 traces); the non-factorable target leaks but is
unsearchable ($2^{128}$, no local gradient). Masking performs
its function at first order on hardware.

\textbf{Artifacts:} five trap classes with gates; the two
platform-specific ones (key-reload bus at $|r|\approx0.6$,
structured-key/roll false recovery at 75.8\%) are, to our
knowledge, unreported for LWC hardware evaluation.

%% ============================================================
%%  IX. CONCLUSION
%% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented an oracle-verified CW305/CW-Husky evaluation
platform for the unmasked NIST LWC ASCON-128 core and three
hardware-verified result classes. First, deep-learning power
analysis recovers round-1 S-box key bits with closed-loop
per-column key recovery at posterior 0.999 in 32 queries, and a
measured $\sqrt{M}$ SNR lever raises rank-1 probability from
0.25 to 0.94 at $M\!=\!64$. Second, clock-glitch fault
injection produces verified single-bit round-11 faults
localized to exact (column, bit) positions in 12 independent
cases spanning 10 distinct state columns — the finest-grained
fault control reported on ASCON-128 hardware — along with a
deterministic output-path fault class and a characterized burst
structure. Third, five leakage-validation gates cover artifact
classes that produced confident false positives during this
campaign, two of which (key-reload bus leakage at
$|r|\approx0.6$, structured-key/free-alignment false recovery
at 75.8\%) are platform-general and, to our knowledge,
unreported. The masked-core comparison confirms first-order
masking effectiveness on the same platform. Full 128-bit key
assembly across all 64 S-box columns is characterized as
ongoing work enabled by the platform.

